<!-- this is just a Doenet version of a WW exercise written by Chrissy Safranski -->

<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="p" from="2" to="4" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="q" from="-4" to="-2" step="1" exclude="abs($p)"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="r" from="-3" to="3" step="1" exclude="abs($p) abs($q) $p $q $0"/>
  
  <function name="f">$p*x^2 + $q*x + $r</function>
  <function name="fP">2*$p*x + $q</function>
  <function name="fPP">2*$p</function>
  <function name="T2">$b0 + $b1*x + $b2*x^2</function>
  <function name="T2P">$b1 + 2*$b2*x</function>
  <function name="T2PP">2*$b2</function>
  <function name="T2Symb">b_0 + b_1*x + b_2*x^2</function>
  <function name="T2PSymb">b_1 + 2*b_2*x</function>
  <function name="T2PPSymb">2b_2</function>

  <math name="a">0</math>
  <math name="b0">$$f($a)</math>
  <math name="b1">$$fP($a)</math>
  <math name="b2">$$fPP($a)/2</math>
</setup>

<p>
  Let <m>f(x) = $f</m> and let <m>T_2(x) = b_0 + b_1x + b_2x^2</m>.  In what follows, we find the values of <m>b_0</m>, <m>b_1</m>, and <m>b_2</m> that make <m>f(x)</m> and <m>T_2(x)</m> have the same height, slope, and concavity at <m>x = 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        Complete the table below by computing derivatives of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>T_2(x)</m> and evaluating them at <m>x = 0</m> as directed.  Initially, several of your responses will involve the unknown coefficients <m>b_0</m>, <m>b_1</m>, and <m>b_2</m>.  Where needed, use underscores to enter the coefficients. For example, to enter <m>b_1</m> type <q>b_1</q>.

              <tabular>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>$f</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>b_0 + b_1x + b_2x^2</m></cell>
                </row>
               <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f1">$fP</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2'(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="T21">$T2PSymb</answer></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f2">$fPP</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2''(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="T22">$T2PPSymb</answer></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>

              <tabular>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="fa">$$f($a)</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="T2a">$$T2Symb($a)</answer></cell>
                </row>
               <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f1a">$$fP($a)</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2'($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="T21a">$$T2PSymb($a)</answer></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f2a">$$fPP($a)</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2''($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="T22a">$$T2PPSymb($a)</answer></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>      
      </p>    
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Now, since we want <m>T_2(x)</m> and <m>f(x)=$f</m> to have the same height at <m>x = $a</m>, we set <m>T_2($a) = f($a)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This implies <m>b_0 = </m><answer name="b0entered">$b0</answer>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, since we want <m>T_2(x)</m> and <m>f(x)=$f</m> to have the same slope at <m>x = $a</m>, we set <m>T_2'($a) = f'($a)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This implies <m>b_1 = </m><answer name="b1entered">$b1</answer>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In addition, since we want <m>T_2(x)</m> and <m>f(x)=$f</m> to have the same concavity at <m>x = $a</m>, we set <m>T_2''($a) = f''($a)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This implies <m>b_2 = </m><answer name="b2entered">$b2</answer>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Putting everything together, the resulting formula for <m>T_2(x)</m> is 
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_2(x) = </m><answer name="T2Entered">$T2</answer>
      </p>
      <p>
        What is true about the relationship between the original quadratic function <m>f(x) = $f</m> and its quadratic approximation <m>T_2(x)</m>?
        <answer>
          <choiceInput>
            <choice><m>f(x) = T_2(x)</m> only when <m>x = $a</m></choice>
            <choice credit="1"><m>f(x) = T_2(x)</m> for every value of <m>x</m></choice>
            <choice><m>f(x) = -T_2(x)</m> for every value of <m>x</m></choice>
          </choiceInput>
        </answer>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>